<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "ads-l", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Our open questions on ADS-L Issue 2.</h1>
  <p class="lede">These are the questions skyBlip puts to EASA on ADS-L 4 SRD-860 Issue 2, where the text leaves a reading open. Each question has a reference, from SB-Q1 to SB-Q8, and says whether it has been sent and answered. Open a question to read the clause, what our firmware does today and the detail of what we ask. Until EASA answers, Go flies the reading described here.</p>
  <p>skyBlip is open-source electronic conspicuity equipment for general aviation. skyBlip Go transmits and receives ADS-L. skyBlip Bravo receives ADS-B on 1090&nbsp;MHz and transmits nothing. Paired with a Go, on the ground, Bravo can name the transponder it takes for the aircraft&rsquo;s own.</p>
</section>

<section class="questions" data-controller="anchored-details" data-action="hashchange@window->anchored-details#open">
  <details id="sb-q1">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q1">SB-Q1</a> &middot; The manufacturer prefix &middot; F.2.2</span>
      <h3>Should the manufacturer prefix sit in the high byte of the address?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.B.3, &ldquo;Bit and Byte Order&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>The following principles apply unless noted otherwise. [&hellip;] Bit numbering: Bits are counted from least significant bit to most significant bit, where the counting starts with zero (0).</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.F.2.2, &ldquo;Sender Address&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Entries 9 and higher of the address mapping table imply a further structuring of the address:</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>The table that follows gives the Manufacturer prefix at offset 0, width 8 bits, and the Base address at offset 8, width 16 bits. The clause continues:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>The combination AMT page + manufacturer prefix shall be uniquely assigned to only one manufacturer. The manufacturer shall use the remaining 16-bit base address space as densely as possible.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Read with B.3, the prefix occupies address bits 0 to 7 and the base address bits 8 to 23. Prefix <code>0xAB</code> with base address <code>0x1234</code> therefore gives address <code>0x1234AB</code>. The outer table follows the same rule: the Address Mapping Table (AMT) entry at offset 0 occupies the sender field&rsquo;s low 6 bits, with the 24-bit address above it.</p>
    <p>We think the manufacturer sub-table has its fields in the wrong order. It appears to list them most significant first, against B.3, without stating an exception. Calling the field a prefix suggests the leading byte. ICAO allocates 24-bit aircraft addresses in blocks defined by their leading bits, and FLARM IDs are commonly written with a leading byte such as <code>DD</code>.</p>
    <p>The registry&rsquo;s uniqueness rule depends on every manufacturer using the same byte. On one AMT page, a manufacturer with prefix <code>0x00</code> in the low byte sends base address <code>0x0101</code> as <code>0x010100</code>. Another with prefix <code>0x01</code> in the high byte sends base address <code>0x0100</code> as <code>0x010100</code>. Two aircraft then share the same AMT and address despite holding different prefixes.</p>
    <p>The ADS-L registry assigned skyBlip manufacturer page 49, AMT entry 58 and prefix 0 on 5 October 2026. skyblip.eu assigns each Go a 16-bit serial at activation, densely from 1. Go puts prefix <code>0x00</code> in the high byte, address bits 16 to 23, and the serial in bits 0 to 15. Serial <code>0x012A</code> sends address <code>0x00012A</code>. This follows the conventional reading of a prefix, not the table as written. Read with B.3, that address has prefix <code>0x2A</code> and base address <code>0x0001</code>. Under the literal reading, each Go&rsquo;s prefix is the low byte of its serial, so units spread across prefixes on page 49 that the registry did not assign to skyBlip. Go keeps the high byte until EASA answers. A change that follows the text as written is prepared and waits for that answer: prefix in bits 0 to 7, serial in bits 8 to 23, and serial <code>0x012A</code> sent as <code>0x012A00</code>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Is the manufacturer prefix intended to occupy the high byte, address bits 16 to 23, with the base address in bits 0 to 15?</strong></p></li>
      <li><p><strong>If so, could a corrigendum or the next issue correct the offsets to base address at 0 and manufacturer prefix at 16?</strong> Alternatively, F.2.2 could state its exception to B.3 explicitly. If the low byte is intended, saying so in the clause would keep manufacturers from reading it two ways.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q2">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q2">SB-Q2</a> &middot; The age of the position &middot; G.1.1 and G.1.16</span>
      <h3>Must every position be a solution measured on a quarter second and sent within 500&nbsp;ms of it?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.G.1.1, &ldquo;Timestamp&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>The Timestamp field indicates the number of quarter seconds since the full hour, modulo 60. [&hellip;] The timestamp refers to the time of the navigation solution (PVT fix) obtained by the GNSS receiver (e.g. on the full second) and included in the Traffic payload. It shall be accurate to &plusmn;&nbsp;10&nbsp;ms.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.G.1.16, &ldquo;Transmit Rate and Timing&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>A message shall be replaced with a new message if the total delay of 500 ms from obtaining (measuring) the PVT fix is exceeded, e.g. due to the frequency being occupied, to prevent old data being sent. No Traffic message shall be sent containing navigation data older than 500 ms.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Read together with the direct slot of C.5, from 450 to 1000&nbsp;ms, every position must be a solution the receiver measured within 10&nbsp;ms of a quarter second, and it must leave within 500&nbsp;ms of that measurement. Go does not move a position forward to a quarter second, because the result would no longer be a solution the receiver obtained.</p>
    <p>This ties each transmission to the receiver&rsquo;s solution schedule, which we have not met in other systems. A receiver solving once a second, on the full second, serves only bursts between 450 and 500&nbsp;ms: 50&nbsp;ms of the 550&nbsp;ms slot. At 5 solutions a second, only the full second falls on a quarter. Only 4 solutions a second, or a multiple, puts every solution on a quarter. Go&rsquo;s receiver, a Quectel L76K, documents 1, 2 and 5 solutions a second, and on the bench it ignored a request for 4. Go therefore runs it at 2, with solutions on .000 and .500 of each second.</p>
    <p>At 2 solutions a second, part of the slot has no usable solution. Go sends on 868.2&nbsp;MHz between 450 and 810&nbsp;ms and on 868.4&nbsp;MHz between 830 and 990&nbsp;ms (<a href="#sb-q3">SB-Q3</a>). The .000 solution serves bursts up to 490&nbsp;ms, keeping 10&nbsp;ms for the burst&rsquo;s air time. The .500 solution reaches Go 40 to 160&nbsp;ms after it was measured. Between 490&nbsp;ms and its arrival, no solution meets both clauses. That gap falls inside the 868.2&nbsp;MHz window. In 26 minutes on the bench, before the change below, 21 of the 81 positions due on 868.2&nbsp;MHz were not sent.</p>
    <p>Go now holds a position that falls in the gap until the .500 solution arrives. If the position&rsquo;s instant has passed by then, Go draws a new instant at random over the rest of the window, from its address and the second, so units do not all transmit on the solution&rsquo;s arrival. A position is lost only if the .500 solution arrives after 810&nbsp;ms, the window&rsquo;s last instant. This is not ideal. On 868.2&nbsp;MHz the bursts no longer spread evenly: about a quarter move from 490&ndash;590&nbsp;ms into 590&ndash;810&nbsp;ms, which then carries about 40&nbsp;% more bursts, and the chance of two units colliding rises from about 2.8&nbsp;% to about 3.9&nbsp;% per neighbour. Every Go has the same receiver and the same gap, so the uneven load adds up across a fleet. It also depends on the receiver&rsquo;s delay, which Go measures but does not control.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Do G.1.1 and G.1.16 intend to bind each position to a solution measured on a quarter second?</strong> Or may a device carry its latest solution forward to the quarter the Timestamp names, with the 500&nbsp;ms counted from that measurement?</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Could a later issue let the Timestamp name the solution&rsquo;s own time more finely than a quarter second?</strong> Receivers solving 1, 5 or 10 times a second could then meet both clauses, and devices could keep an even spread over the slot.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q3">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q3">SB-Q3</a> &middot; The timing &middot; C.2, C.2.5, C.5, G.1.16 and G.2.11</span>
      <h3>Is the transmit timing Go uses valid under ADS-L?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.C.5, &ldquo;Time Multiplexing&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Transmissions must be completed before the end of the slot. [&hellip;] Direct: Reserved for payloads of immediate relevance, urgency and with implications to safety, transmitted from an airborne ADS-L client. This slot lasts from 450 ms to 1000 ms, relative to the full UTC seconds.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.C.2.5, &ldquo;Frequency Diversity&rdquo;</strong>, says that &ldquo;An ADS-L sender is free to select either frequency for transmission&rdquo;, except that Traffic and Status alternate. Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.G.1.16, &ldquo;Transmit Rate and Timing&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>If the flight state is &ldquo;On ground&rdquo;, the transmit rate shall be reduced to 0.1 Hz (once every 10 seconds).</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Clause C.2 sets the backoff interval to 15 to 250&nbsp;ms, and clause G.2.11 sets the Status&rsquo;s minimum rate to once per 20 seconds.</p>
    <p>Go has one radio, so it listens to one channel at a time. It divides each second into three listening windows: the uplink on 869.525&nbsp;MHz from 210 to 440&nbsp;ms, 868.2&nbsp;MHz from 450 to 820&nbsp;ms and 868.4&nbsp;MHz from 830 to 1200&nbsp;ms. The two 868&nbsp;MHz windows follow the channels FLARM-generation devices use in each second, so Go hears both systems. Go transmits only on the channel it is listening to. Positions and Status start between 450 and 810&nbsp;ms on 868.2&nbsp;MHz and between 830 and 990&nbsp;ms on 868.4&nbsp;MHz, so every burst ends before 1000&nbsp;ms. The callsign goes after the slot, which <a href="#sb-q6">SB-Q6</a> asks about.</p>

    <%= render "shared/second" %>

    <p>Each channel therefore gets part of the direct slot, not all of it. The channel follows the parity of the UTC second, so in a given second every Go in flight sends its position on the same channel. Two 4.64&nbsp;ms bursts overlap when their starts fall within 5&nbsp;ms of each other. Over the 360&nbsp;ms of start instants on 868.2&nbsp;MHz, that gives about 2.8&nbsp;% per neighbour, or 3.9&nbsp;% with the redraw of <a href="#sb-q2">SB-Q2</a>. Over the 160&nbsp;ms on 868.4&nbsp;MHz, it gives about 6&nbsp;%. Spread over the whole 550&nbsp;ms slot, it would be about 1.8&nbsp;%.</p>
    <p>A burst that listen-before-talk defers stays in its window and on its channel. Go draws the wait from C.2&rsquo;s 15 to 250&nbsp;ms, cut at the window&rsquo;s last instant, so on 868.4&nbsp;MHz a wait never exceeds 160&nbsp;ms. A burst with less than 15&nbsp;ms of its window left is missed. On the ground it tries again 2&nbsp;s later, on the same channel. In flight a missed position is not retried, because the next one follows a second later.</p>
    <p>Each Go follows a 20&nbsp;s cycle. It starts at an even second set by the unit&rsquo;s serial, <span class="mono">c = 2 &times; (serial mod 10)</span>, so a fleet spreads over ten start seconds. On the ground, Go schedules a position at c on 868.2&nbsp;MHz and at c + 9 on 868.4&nbsp;MHz. It schedules a Status at c + 2 on 868.4&nbsp;MHz and at c + 11 on 868.2&nbsp;MHz, and its callsign at c + 4 and c + 14. In flight it adds a position in each of the other seconds and keeps the Status and callsign seconds.</p>

    <%= render "shared/cycle" %>

    <p>Ground positions are 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart, one every 10&nbsp;s on average. At exactly 10&nbsp;s, the parity rule would put two positions in a row on one channel, which C.2.5 forbids. The odd gap also spreads a fleet evenly: across the ten start seconds, each channel window of the 20&nbsp;s carries one ground burst. Each channel carries a Status every 20&nbsp;s, so a receiver that hears one channel still gets G.2.11&rsquo;s rate. The <%= link_to_page "timing", label: "timing guide" %> gives each rule in full.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Does transmitting on each channel in only part of the direct slot meet C.5 and C.2.5?</strong> Or does ADS-L expect a sender to spread its bursts on both channels over the whole slot, from 450 to 1000&nbsp;ms?</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Does a ground position every 9 then 11&nbsp;s meet G.1.16&rsquo;s 0.1&nbsp;Hz?</strong> Or must ground positions be exactly 10&nbsp;s apart?</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>May a deferred burst miss its turn when less than 15&nbsp;ms of its window is left?</strong> Or should the backoff reach past the window, up to the end of the direct slot?</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Does any other part of this timing conflict with ADS-L as EASA reads it?</strong></p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q4">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q4">SB-Q4</a> &middot; Channel alternation &middot; C.2.5</span>
      <h3>After a burst that did not go out, may the next one keep its scheduled channel?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.C.2.5, &ldquo;Frequency Diversity&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>For the &ldquo;Traffic&rdquo; and &ldquo;Status&rdquo; payloads (see Subpart G), the sender shall alternate between these frequencies for every transmission to facilitate reception of these payloads for restricted, single-channel receivers.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Go picks the channel from the parity of the UTC second: a position on 868.2&nbsp;MHz in an even second and on 868.4&nbsp;MHz in an odd one, and the Status the other way round. When a burst does not go out, because the channel stayed busy, the next one keeps its own second&rsquo;s channel, so two positions in a row can leave on the same frequency. A takeoff or a landing changes the rate, and in about one case in two it puts two positions in a row on one channel, once. A single-channel receiver still hears Go every other second, which is the purpose the clause gives.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Does alternation by the schedule meet C.2.5?</strong> Or must each transmission take the channel opposite to the last one that actually went out?</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q5">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q5">SB-Q5</a> &middot; The ICAO address &middot; F.2.3</span>
      <h3>Should every payload carry the pilot&rsquo;s ICAO address, and which settings must Go refuse?</h3>
      <span class="note">Asked: [asked on date]. No answer from EASA yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.F.2.3, &ldquo;ICAO Address&rdquo;</strong>, in <a href="https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/document-library/agency-decisions/ed-decision-2022024r">ADS-L 4 SRD-860, ED Decision 2022/024/R, Issue 2</a>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>For this setting, the ICAO address shall be configurable and entered by the user of the ADS-L system, thereby overriding the address given by the manufacturer. This is the preferred mode for aircraft that have an ICAO address (Mode-S transponder code). This setting shall be revertible by user configuration. For aircraft that concurrently transmit using other e-conspicuity systems such as ADS-B, or aircraft that use a Mode-S transponder, it shall use this type of address to allow for disambiguation and attribution of the different radio transmissions on the receiver side. The ADS-L system shall enforce this by disallowing inconsistent user configurations.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Today Go uses the pilot&rsquo;s configured ICAO address on every payload and refuses an address no aircraft holds (<code>FFFFFF</code>) or an entered address that conflicts with the transponder Bravo identifies on the ground.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Should every payload use the ICAO address?</strong> When the pilot enters the aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address, Go sends every payload under Address Mapping Table (AMT) entry 5, ICAO, with that address. This includes Traffic, Status and payload type 66, which carries the aircraft&rsquo;s registration.</p>
        <p>Go no longer transmits under skyBlip&rsquo;s manufacturer address: AMT entry 58, manufacturer page 49, prefix 0, with the unit&rsquo;s serial in the remaining 16 bits. The ADS-L registry assigned that range to skyBlip. Clearing the ICAO setting restores AMT entry 58 and the unit&rsquo;s address from the next burst. Is this the intent of F.2.3, or should some payloads stay under the manufacturer&rsquo;s address?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>What must &ldquo;disallowing inconsistent user configurations&rdquo; cover?</strong> Go refuses <code>FFFFFF</code>, an address no aircraft holds. On the ground, while a paired Bravo names the transponder it takes for the aircraft&rsquo;s own, Go also refuses an entered address that differs from it.</p>
        <p>Go does not refuse an unset ICAO address when Bravo identifies a Mode-S transponder on the aircraft. It proposes the address Bravo hears and lets the pilot accept it. Does F.2.3 require Go to refuse that configuration, or to enforce the address itself? Are there other configurations EASA intends the device to disallow?</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q6">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q6">SB-Q6</a> &middot; The callsign &middot; C.5 and F.2.1</span>
      <h3>May payload type 66 carry the callsign after the direct slot, or could EASA share a preview of the callsign in Issue 3 instead?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.C.5</strong> gives aircraft the direct slot, from 450 to 1000&nbsp;ms of each UTC second, and says of the rest of the second:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Reserved: The unused time is reserved for future extensions and shall not be used for any ADS-L transmissions.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.F.2.1, &ldquo;Payload Type Identifier&rdquo;</strong>, lists payload type 66 as &ldquo;OGN Diagnostics&rdquo; and says its &ldquo;Payload definition is outside the scope of this document.&rdquo;</p>
    <p>Go sends its callsign in OGN&rsquo;s payload type 66 registration record, on the ADS-L frame, between 0 and 200&nbsp;ms of the second after its slot. The <%= link_to_page "ads-l/callsign", label: "callsign page" %> explains that record. On 2 October 2026, EASA told us orally that Issue 3 will carry a callsign. The callsign lives outside Go&rsquo;s ADS-L stack, so a different answer changes the callsign code and not the ADS-L version.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Is a payload the specification does not define an ADS-L transmission in the sense of C.5?</strong> We read it as not being one. Or does payload type 66 belong in the direct slot?</p></li>
      <li><p><strong>Could EASA share a preview of how Issue 3 carries the callsign?</strong> skyBlip would then prepare that encoding and stop sending payload type 66 once Issue 3 is published, and the first question would no longer arise.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q7">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q7">SB-Q7</a> &middot; The flight state &middot; G.1.2</span>
      <h3>Should a craft that can hover announce Undefined rather than On ground?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.G.1.2, &ldquo;Flight State&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>The &ldquo;Undefined&rdquo; value shall be used if no reasonable decision is possible by the ADS-L device.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Go decides the flight state from ground speed. A paraglider soaring a ridge into wind, a balloon or a hovering helicopter can fly with no ground speed, so for those aircraft categories Go cannot tell a landing from flight. For categories 3, 5, 7 to 13 and 15 to 17 it sends Undefined instead of On ground. It therefore keeps the 1&nbsp;Hz rate of G.1.16 instead of dropping to the ground rate of 0.1&nbsp;Hz.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Is this the use of Undefined the clause intends?</strong> Or should these categories announce On ground whenever their ground speed is low, and transmit at the ground rate?</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>

  <details id="sb-q8">
    <summary>
      <span class="kicker"><a href="#sb-q8">SB-Q8</a> &middot; Velocity accuracy &middot; G.1.16 and G.1.17</span>
      <h3>May a device with no measured velocity bound send its velocity accuracy as Unknown?</h3>
      <span class="note">Not sent yet.</span>
    </summary>
    <p>Clause <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.G.1.17, &ldquo;Quality Indicators&rdquo;</strong>, says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Unlike the ADS-B certification approach (CS-STAN Configuration 3 in particular), uncertified ADS-L devices shall however not report all quality values as 0. Instead, they shall report their positioning performance dynamically as defined in the previous sections.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Go&rsquo;s receiver reports its horizontal and vertical dilution of precision, but no velocity error. Go&rsquo;s horizontal and vertical accuracy change with each fix, while its velocity accuracy is sent as 0, &ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;, which the table of G.1.16, &ldquo;Velocity Accuracy&rdquo;, allows.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p><strong>Does that meet G.1.17?</strong> Not every quality value is 0, but the velocity accuracy always is.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </details>
</section>

